{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And He set them</b>. It did not suffice to say ״וַיְהִי כֵן״, because He wished to inform us that HaShem gave this matter as a gift of gratuitous grace, to illuminate the world, as they said of blessed memory in the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 6) — see there. And the intention in this is that He informed them that they do not illuminate the world by virtue of being worthy of light, for if so, whenever the luminaries see that the inhabitants of the world are corrupting their ways they would gather in their glow. Moreover, on account of some cause they might wish to gather in their glow, as they said in the Midrash (Proem to Lamentations Rabbah) that Moses our master, peace be upon him, said to the sun at the time of the Temple's destruction: ״Why did you not darken your light when the enemy entered the sanctuary״ and so forth. Therefore HaShem said that they are set to illuminate upon the earth, and from now on they are not within their own jurisdiction and are compelled in their function to illuminate, and no cause can prevent it. And this opened our eyes to why Scripture repeated a second time to say <b>and to rule the day</b> — had He not already said ״הַמָּאוֹר הַגָּדוֹל לְמֶמְשֶׁלֶת הַיּוֹם״ and so forth? And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 6) interpreted that Scripture alludes to the righteous — see there for their words. But according to the plain sense there is no need to repeat and say it, and according to our approach it was necessary to repeat and say it on account of His saying ״וַיִּתֵּן״ — for they are given, given, from now on: behold, they are not within their own jurisdiction but are bound by another's obligation upon them. Therefore He repeated and said that their dominion stands in its place — except specifically with regard to this function of illuminating, it is not in any hand to change; but as for the other dominions that exist in the day and the night that are assigned to them — they are the ones that rule." "<b>And the reason</b> that He repeated further to say <b>and to divide</b> — here He commanded them not to enter one into the boundary of the other, and each one is commanded regarding its own border not to transgress, for by this the distinction of day from night will be recognizable."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "Resolved ד׳/ה׳ as HaShem per voice rules. The final clause's syntax is compressed; rendered hyper-literally to preserve the distinction he draws between the luminaries' fixed function of illumination versus their other variable dominions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T18:56:12.138926088Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}